WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:06.808
Unless means “if not.” When the unless condition is absent, the other part of the sentence must be true.

2
00:00:07.108 --> 00:00:10.351
The survey continues unless the hillside is unsafe.

3
00:00:10.651 --> 00:00:12.805
Suppose the hillside is not unsafe.

4
00:00:13.105 --> 00:00:16.476
The stated exception is absent, so the survey continues.

5
00:00:16.776 --> 00:00:19.165
Now suppose the survey does not continue.

6
00:00:19.465 --> 00:00:22.751
The exception must be present: the hillside is unsafe.

7
00:00:23.051 --> 00:00:27.531
But an unsafe hillside alone does not tell us whether the survey continues.

8
00:00:27.831 --> 00:00:30.327
The rule has not made that reverse guarantee.

9
00:00:30.627 --> 00:00:33.827
The LSAT can express a requirement without only if.

10
00:00:34.127 --> 00:00:41.423
Translating both equivalent forms lets you recognize that requirement even when an answer starts from the other idea.

11
00:00:41.723 --> 00:00:45.819
The two ideas are the survey continues and the hillside is unsafe.

12
00:00:46.119 --> 00:00:48.657
Keep any existing negative or restriction.

13
00:00:48.957 --> 00:00:51.624
Negate your chosen idea and put it on the left.

14
00:00:51.924 --> 00:00:56.383
Before using an exception, say what the sentence guarantees when it is absent.

15
00:00:56.683 --> 00:01:03.168
With until, retain the time boundary and leave later behavior open unless a second statement settles it.

16
00:01:03.468 --> 00:01:08.524
Begin with accurate translation; optional timed Unless practice comes afterward.
